<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  🔴 The ONE layout behind BOTH floating pickers — the snippet picker (issue
  #38) and the history picker. A search field over a scrolling list, with one
  place a reason is shown when there are no rows.

  It replaced activity_snip_picker.xml rather than being copied from it. Every
  argument below is a rule the two pickers must AGREE on — the window-sizing
  contract, the fixed list height, the single empty-state slot — and a second
  copy of a rule that must agree is how the width defect this file's own
  comment describes happened in the first place.

  🔴 The HEADING and the search HINT are set in CODE, from
  FloatingPickerActivity.titleRes / searchHintRes, because they are the one
  thing that genuinely differs per picker. There is deliberately no
  android:text/android:hint here: a default would render for a frame before the
  subclass's own string replaced it, and (worse) a subclass that forgot to
  declare one would silently show the other picker's.

  🔴 A floating Activity rather than a view in the bubble's overlay window, for
  two independent reasons written out on FloatingPickerActivity: a search field
  needs IME focus, which the bubble's FLAG_NOT_FOCUSABLE window cannot give it
  without stealing focus from the app underneath; and an Activity's lifecycle
  removes the late-async-load defect class the chip reveal needed a generation
  counter for.

  Built from the same four conventions as activity_settings.xml and from no new
  ones: @drawable/input_bg on the field, @color/dicta_on_surface for text,
  @style/Text.Dicta.SectionHeading for the heading, and a framework ListView
  (NOT a RecyclerView — the app has zero runtime dependencies by decision; see
  app/build.gradle.kts).

  🔴 EVERY `match_parent` BELOW MATCHES THE WINDOW, NOT THE SCREEN, and the
  window is only screen-sized because CODE makes it so. @style/Theme.Dicta.Overlay
  is a floating dialog theme, so this window is WRAP_CONTENT in both axes until
  FloatingPickerActivity.onCreate calls FloatingWindow.applySize(this) — which
  sets the width to @fraction/overlay_width_fraction of the display. Without
  that call the window's width is its widest wrap_content child, i.e. the
  heading TextView directly below, and the whole card renders about a fifth of
  the screen wide with every label truncated to ~6 characters. That is not a
  hypothetical: it is what 0.4.0 did on a Galaxy S21. A `match_parent` here is
  therefore a claim about a window that something else has to size — and the
  reason that call now lives in the SHELL is that remembering it per activity
  is the obligation that was already missed once.

  🔴 The list's height is a FIXED 320dp, and that is what the file says
  because it is what the file does. It is deliberately NOT wrap_content:
  wrap_content asks a ListView to measure every row, which both defeats
  recycling and unbounds the window — a 1000-row history would grow the window
  past the screen.

  It is also not `0dp` + layout_weight="1", which an earlier version of this
  comment described and the file never implemented. The weighted design needs a
  parent with a bounded HEIGHT to divide up, and this root's height is
  wrap_content inside a floating dialog window whose height stays content-driven
  (FloatingWindow.applySize caps the WIDTH only, and passes WRAP_CONTENT for the
  height deliberately). A weighted child in a wrap_content parent resolves to
  zero height, i.e. an invisible list.

  🔴 The earlier version of this paragraph argued the fixed height partly on
  "there is no device in this loop to measure one on". THAT IS NO LONGER TRUE —
  0.4.0 was driven on a Galaxy S21 and the list was confirmed to scroll
  correctly at 320dp — so the fixed cap is kept on its own merits (a bounded
  height the ListView can recycle against) and not for want of a device. Nobody
  has measured a better number; if you change it, measure it on hardware and say
  which. ⚠ That measurement is the SNIPPET picker's; the history picker's rows
  are a different height and nothing on hardware has looked at them yet.

  The cost, stated: at a large font scale 320dp shows fewer rows rather than
  growing. The list scrolls, so nothing is unreachable.
-->
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:orientation="vertical"
    android:padding="20dp">

    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/picker_title"
        style="@style/Text.Dicta.SectionHeading"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content" />

    <EditText
        android:id="@+id/picker_search"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="48dp"
        android:layout_marginTop="12dp"
        android:background="@drawable/input_bg"
        android:importantForAutofill="no"
        android:inputType="text"
        android:maxLines="1"
        android:paddingStart="12dp"
        android:paddingEnd="12dp"
        android:textColor="@color/dicta_on_surface"
        android:textColorHint="@color/dicta_on_surface_variant"
        android:textSize="15sp" />

    <!-- The one place a reason is shown when there are no rows. Three distinct
         reasons (unreadable / nothing stored / nothing matches) share this view
         and never share a string — see FloatingPickerActivity.render. No
         android:text: the shell sets it on every render, and a default here
         would be one picker's copy showing in the other. -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/picker_empty"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="16dp"
        android:textColor="@color/dicta_on_surface_variant"
        android:textSize="15sp"
        android:visibility="gone" />

    <ListView
        android:id="@+id/picker_list"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="320dp"
        android:layout_marginTop="12dp"
        android:divider="@null"
        android:dividerHeight="0dp"
        android:overScrollMode="never"
        android:visibility="gone" />
</LinearLayout>
